### Reviewer 3: code quality and robustness

#### Original review

```
M17 reviewer 3: code quality. Math++ worktree review3, HEAD a6b9c78, 2026-11-20
==========================================================================================

WHAT WAS RUN
- cargo test --workspace (debug): 1431 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored. 510 s wall including the build.
  Slowest suites: mpp-ide tests/m16.rs 112 s, mpp-export tests/golden.rs 72 s, mpp-plot visual_calculus 24 s.
  Two tests ran past 60 s (every_example_exports_to_a_readable_pdf, every_example_has_a_screenshot_and_no_raw_source).
  No flaky failures in one run. cargo-llvm-cov is not installed, so coverage was not measured.
- cargo deny check licenses: "licenses ok" (one warning: Unicode-DFS-2016 is allowed but never used).
- cargo deny check advisories: FAILED, with RUSTSEC-2026-0187 (lopdf stack overflow, dev-dependency only) and
  RUSTSEC-2026-0192 (ttf-parser unmaintained; a direct dependency of mpp-render).
- grep for unwrap( / expect( / panic! / unreachable! / todo! in non-test code (after #[cfg(test)] is cut off):
  0 unwrap, 0 expect, 1 panic! (tools/testkit.rs:148, inside a test-only module), 1 unreachable!
  (mpp-syntax/src/math/parser.rs:90, which really can't be reached: the scoped thread either ran the closure or never took it).
- unsafe: none. #![forbid(unsafe_code)] is set in 7 of the 9 crates (missing from mpp-syntax and mpp-render, see Minor).
- Fuzzing (scratch only, nothing committed):
  * a proptest harness (scratch prop/tests/fuzz.rs) running parse_document, parse_math/parse_expr, render_html and
    Session::update on 3000 LaTeX-fragment soups each, plus arbitrary Unicode (\PC{0,120}), in debug and release: 0 panics.
  * 400 random documents through the debug `mpp run`: 0 panics, 0 hangs.
  * Hand-made hostile inputs through debug `mpp run` and `mpp export --to pdf|tex|html`: 1400 unary minuses, a
    700-level power chain, 500 nested brackets, 300 nested \frac, 400 nested \sqrt, a 20 000-term sum, 10^15-term sums,
    10^9 matrix powers, factorials of 10^20, mod 0, divcount(0), recursive and circular definitions, a 100 000th sequence
    term, rk4 with 10^12 steps, truncated input, combining marks, zero-width spaces.
    Every one gave a clean error or answer. The only slow one was factor(2^128+1) (M1).
    nextprime(10^150), isprime(2^511-1), totient(2^200+1) and the solve of a degree-50 polynomial all returned within 2 s.
- clippy -W pedantic (+indexing_slicing, arithmetic_side_effects) on mpp-syntax, mpp-proof and mpp-export: mostly
  must_use and docs noise. 126 indexing and 11 slicing sites were flagged; the ones checked are bounds-checked,
  and fuzzing never reached one. Not required, no action.

Overall: error handling is unusually disciplined. No reachable panic on user input was found, and there is no unsafe code.

BLOCKERS (0)
------------
None found.

MAJOR (7)
---------
M1. CPU-bound work has no deadline or cancellation, so one short integer input stalls evaluation for over a minute.
    crates/mpp-core/src/domains/number_theory.rs:19,195-215,219 (RHO_LIMIT = 2 000 000 steps for each of 19 values of c,
    BigInt gcd every step). Test: `$$ \operatorname{factor}(2^{128}+1) = ? $$` took 80 s in the debug `mpp run`
    (measured with the machine otherwise loaded), then failed with "no factor found in time". Release is faster but
    still has no limit on wall time. Other step-capped loops can be slow in the same way.
    In the IDE, explicit `= ?` queries run on the single eval worker (crates/mpp-ide/src/eval/mod.rs:93-140) with no
    budget and no way to abort, so every later edit's results freeze until the slow job finishes.
    Fix: give Ctx a deadline or cancel flag (an Arc<AtomicBool> plus an Instant) and check it in the long loops
    (rho, trial division, Wynn/numeric sums, integration recursion, expand). Cap rho at a total step budget across
    all c values (for example 200k with Brent's batched gcd) and return MathError::TooLarge.
    The eval worker should set a cancel flag when a newer job arrives.

M2. Auto-evaluation starts one uncancellable 256 MB-stack thread for each distinct slow job, with no limit on how many.
    crates/mpp-eval/src/auto.rs:183-200. A job that misses the budget keeps running in a detached thread until it is done.
    Typing a slow expression one character at a time makes a new job key (and a new thread) for each prefix,
    so CPU load and reserved address space grow with typing.
    Fix: a bounded pool (1 or 2 workers) with a queue, newest job first. Superseded jobs are dropped or cancelled
    through the same cancel flag as M1.

M3. The Lean bridge can deadlock on a full pipe. crates/mpp-proof/src/lean.rs:929-955.
    stdout and stderr are piped, but they are only read after try_wait() reports the exit. When `lake build` prints
    more than the pipe buffer holds (a Mathlib build or a long error list easily does), the child blocks on write.
    The loop then runs to config.timeout and reports "TimedOut" instead of the real errors.
    The same path is used by the agent's lean_check tool (agent/tools.rs:302).
    Fix: drain both pipes on two threads (or merge them into one) while polling, then join them after the exit.

M4. Duplicated maths engines from parallel lanes, with a sniffing dispatcher added on top.
    - Two Laplace engines (table, partial-fraction inverse, constant-coefficient ODE method):
      crates/mpp-core/src/tools/multivar/laplace.rs and crates/mpp-core/src/tools_b/ode/laplace.rs.
    - Two rk4: domains/ode.rs:336 and tools_b/numerics.rs:513. Two newton: domains/solve/numeric.rs:89 and
      tools_b/numerics.rs:296. Two dsolve front ends: domains/ode.rs:454 and tools_b/ode.
    - crates/mpp-eval/src/dispatch.rs:1-15 says why it exists: it reads the argument shape to route `dsolve`, `rk4`
      and `laplace` to the "right" duplicate, because registration order used to decide.
    - Module names record lane history, not purpose: tools/ vs tools_b/, helpers/ vs helpers_b.rs.
    Risk: fixes land in one copy only, and the two copies can give different answers to the same question.
    Fix: keep one implementation of each (the more general one), make the other a thin adapter for its argument form,
    delete dispatch.rs's shape sniffing, and rename tools_b/helpers_b by subject.

M5. The `Domain` extension point is not pluggable without touching core and eval.
    crates/mpp-core/src/domains/mod.rs:150-168 (Registry::standard hard-codes 15 domains).
    crates/mpp-eval/src/domains_wire.rs:66-69 (a private static OnceLock<Registry> built from standard(), with no way to
    inject). A new field also needs edits in fields_wire.rs (textbook names), dispatch.rs and the IDE insert catalogue.
    The trait itself is clean: name, operators, apply, returning Option so the next domain can answer.
    Fix: let Session or Holders own a Registry (Session::with_registry / register_domain), and pass it through Ctx
    instead of the global. Optionally a Domain::textbook_names() hook so fields_wire reads the table from the domains.
    Then a new field is one new crate or module plus one register call.

M6. The installer ships no third-party notices for Rust crates.
    work/release/build.ps1:36-38 copies only work/release/licences (font licences) plus Math++'s own licences.
    The binaries statically link several hundred MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD and ISC crates (eframe/egui, reqwest, rustls or
    native-tls, svg2pdf, resvg, num-*, ...). Those licences require their copyright and licence text to ship with
    binary copies.
    Fix: generate THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html with cargo-about (or `cargo deny list` plus a template) in build.ps1,
    stage it in licences\, and add it to install-test.ps1's file checks.

M7. Big-stack setup only works for the Windows binaries.
    crates/mpp-cli/build.rs and crates/mpp-ide/build.rs set /STACK:256 MB for Windows bins only. The comment says
    page layout and the parser's callers "walk maths nested 256 levels deep or 10 000 operators long on the main thread".
    On Linux and macOS (eframe is built with x11/wayland, so other platforms are intended), the main thread keeps its
    default 8 MB, and library users of mpp-render/mpp-export keep whatever stack their caller has.
    Not reproduced here (Windows only), so treat as a portability risk.
    Fix: run the CLI's command body and the IDE's page build on a std::thread with an explicit stack_size, as
    session.rs already does for eval. Or depth-limit page layout to what the parser's SAFE_DEPTH (64) allows on a
    normal stack. Then delete the linker flags.

MINOR (13)
----------
m1. crates/mpp-cli/src/main.rs:307: `mpp key set` prints "(it is not shown)", but read_line echoes the key to the terminal
    and its scrollback. Fix: read it with the rpassword crate (or the console API with echo off), or change the prompt text.
m2. crates/mpp-proof/Cargo.toml:15 and agent/key.rs:47: keyring 3 is built with only `windows-native`. On macOS and Linux,
    keyring 3 then falls back to its in-memory mock store, so `key set` reports success and the key is gone at exit.
    The doc comment claims Keychain and Secret Service. Fix: add apple-native and sync-secret-service features per target,
    or say "Windows only" and refuse elsewhere.
    Apart from this, secrets handling is good: ApiKey has a redacted Debug and no Display, the transport takes the key
    out of band, errors go through scrub(), the IDE field is password-masked and cleared with mem::take, settings.json
    stores only agent and environment ids, and no real key is in git history (only the fake test key sk-ant-test-...).
m3. work/release/build.ps1:70,74: file and folder names go into the WiX XML without XML escaping. A docs or examples file
    with & < > " in its name breaks candle. Fix: [Security.SecurityElement]::Escape() on Name and Source.
m4. work/release/install-test.ps1:72: the script prints "RESULT: n failure(s)" but always exits 0, so CI can't gate on it.
    It also runs with ErrorActionPreference Continue when no .msi is found. It installs into the real user profile and
    its uninstall step would remove a real Math++ install. Fix: `exit $fail`, throw when $msi is null, and refuse to run
    when Math++ is already installed.
m5. deny.toml: there are no [advisories] or [bans] sections, so `cargo deny check` (all checks) fails today.
    Fix: add an [advisories] section, ignore RUSTSEC-2026-0187 for the dev-only lopdf with a reason (or bump lopdf),
    track the ttf-parser replacement (skrifa/read-fonts are already in the tree through resvg), and drop the unused
    Unicode-DFS-2016. The single licence exception (epaint_default_fonts, Ubuntu Font Licence 1.0) is narrow and
    documented, which is fine. It is already flagged NEEDS BEN.
m6. crates/mpp-cli/Cargo.toml:23: the CLI depends on mpp-ide only for `mpp ide`, so mpp.exe links the whole GUI
    (73 MB debug) and duplicates Math++.exe. Fix: put it behind an optional `ide` feature, or have `mpp ide` launch
    Math++.exe found next to it.
m7. crates/mpp-syntax/src/lib.rs and crates/mpp-render/src/lib.rs don't have #![forbid(unsafe_code)] (they contain no unsafe).
    Fix: add it for consistency, or set `unsafe_code = "forbid"` once under [workspace.lints.rust].
m8. crates/mpp-cli/build.rs and crates/mpp-ide/build.rs are copies of each other except for 3 strings. Fix: a shared
    helper (a small build-dependency crate or an include!d file).
m9. crates/mpp-eval/build.rs: a missing std/ folder silently gives an empty standard library (unwrap_or_default), with no
    warning. That breaks a packaged crate. Fix: emit cargo:warning, or fail the build, when std/ is missing or empty.
m10. crates/mpp-ide/src/proof/ui.rs:639: lean::find_lake() walks every PATH entry with is_file() on each repaint of the
    alignment window. Fix: cache the result (OnceLock, or refresh it when the window opens).
m11. crates/mpp-export/src/tex.rs:695: display maths source goes into the .tex as written (after texify_math). A shared
    .mpp can carry \input, \openout or \write18 into a .tex someone compiles. It is harmless with default pdflatex
    (restricted shell-escape), but worth a guard. Fix: drop or neutralise \input, \include, \openout, \write and
    \immediate in texify_math.
m12. Layering and god-crate: mpp-core is 55k lines (CAS, 16 domains, units, display formatting, tools and tools_b).
    mpp-eval runs 15 holder evaluators in a first-match chain whose order matters (holders.rs:209-225).
    Fix (later): split mpp-core into a CAS core plus a domains crate, and document the holder order or make holders
    declare the names they claim, as Domain::operators does.
m13. The Lean and agent file tools check `..` and absolute paths (agent/tools.rs:251-262) but not symlinks inside the
    document folder. Low risk. Fix: canonicalize and check starts_with(folder).

TEST-COVERAGE NOTES
- No test calls lean::build with output bigger than the pipe buffer (see M3).
- No test runs a query that exceeds a time budget on the explicit `= ?` path, or checks thread counts (M1, M2).
- install-test.ps1 checks GUST and OFL but not UFL-Ubuntu-font.txt, Hack-LICENSE.txt or emoji-icon-font-MIT.txt.
- Suggest adding the proptest harness above (parse, eval and render never panic) to mpp-eval/tests. It ran in 6 s release
  and 44 s debug at 3000 cases.
```

#### First re-check

```
M17 reviewer 3 re-check: code quality. Worktree review3, HEAD 8288a5e (fix rounds since a6b9c78), 2026-11-21
==============================================================================================================

SUMMARY
- Majors 7: 6 FIXED, 1 FIXED with an accepted risk (M7). None NOT FIXED, none REGRESSED.
- Minors 13: 10 FIXED, 3 NOT FIXED (m6, m8, m12; all low priority, m12 was already marked "later").
- New in the fix-round code: 1 Major (N1, the high-precision pi/ln2 cache can be poisoned), 5 Minors.
- Blockers: none.
- cargo deny check (every check): advisories ok, bans ok, licenses ok, sources ok. Only duplicate-version warnings.
- cargo test --workspace (debug): 1599 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored, 564 s wall including the build (run 1).
  Second run for flakiness: see TESTS below.

MAJORS
------
M1 cancellation and deadlines: FIXED.
  mpp_core::budget (new): a thread-local Budget (an Arc<AtomicBool> cancel flag plus an optional deadline),
  installed by Session::update on the eval thread and by each auto-eval pool worker. check() is called in rho,
  trial division, numeric sums, integration (numeric, improper, multiple), series, expand, ODE steppers,
  rk4_march and resolve. rho now has one total budget of 600 000 steps for all c. CLI: 60 s per query.
  IDE: a newer edit sets the cancel flag (eval/mod.rs:269). hp module: tick() in the long loops,
  approx() returns None once stopped() is set.
  Residuals (Minor, see N3 and N4): the IDE sets no time limit for explicit `= ?` queries (only cancel by editing);
  a small race can clear a cancel; deadlines can overshoot by seconds inside hp.
  Nit: number_theory.rs:194 says "Brent's cycle detection" but the loop is Floyd's (x, then f(f(y))).

M2 auto-eval pool: FIXED.
  auto.rs: a pool of POOL_SIZE = 2 workers, a queue of MAX_QUEUE = 8 run newest first, the oldest dropped, one
  cancel flag per job, workers that exit when idle, a 120 s WORKER_LIMIT, and sweep() cancels jobs whose text is gone.
  Cancelled results are not cached.

M3 Lean pipes: FIXED.
  lean.rs run_capturing drains stdout and stderr on two threads while it polls, and kills the child on time-out.
  There is a unit test that runs it (lean.rs:1102, 1114).
  Residual (Minor): on time-out only `lake` is killed. Grandchild `lean` processes inherit the pipe handles,
  so out.join() can wait until they exit, past the time-out.

M4 duplicate engines and dispatch by shape: FIXED.
  tools_b/ and helpers_b are gone, and modules are named by subject (calc/, ode/, transforms/, helpers/).
  - Laplace: one engine, transforms/laplace.rs, with the ODE method in ode/laplace_method.rs.
    helpers/transforms.rs is only a front end.
  - RK4: one scalar stepper, numerics::rk4_march. domains/ode.rs::rk4 and numerics::rk4 both call it.
  - Newton: one loop, numerics::newton_iterate. solve/numeric.rs::newton calls it with a central difference.
  - dsolve: one, ode/mod.rs::dsolve. helpers/ode.rs::dsolve only reads the arguments.
  - dispatch.rs is now a clear table. FORMS lists the operators, the accepted form in words, a predicate and the
    answerer (Registry, Toolkit, Multivar, FromTo, Transform or Pass). The first matching row wins.
    The module doc says each engine exists once. Insert-menu aliases are a separate ALIASES table.
    There are tests for form_of.
  Small leftovers (not worth a finding): domains/ode.rs::rk4_system keeps its own vector RK4 step (lines 202-218),
  so "every RK4 answer comes from rk4_march" is not true for systems. mpp-plot helpers.rs:484
  newton_iterates is a third Newton loop, for drawing only, with its own stopping rule.

M5 Session-owned registry: FIXED.
  Holders owns an Arc<Registry>. There are Session::register_domain, Session::with_registry,
  Holders::set_domains and Registry::register.
  domains_wire::registry() (the global OnceLock) is still used in one place: matrix arithmetic
  (matadd and so on, domains_wire.rs:947), which only needs the standard matrix operators. It is documented.
  There is no Domain::textbook_names hook (that part was optional).

M6 third-party notices: FIXED.
  work/release/third-party-notices.ps1 builds THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt from `cargo metadata`, using only normal
  (non-dev, non-build) dependencies for the target. build.ps1 calls it and throws when the file is missing.
  install-test.ps1 checks the file, as well as the UFL, Hack and emoji licences.
  Ran it: 302 crates, 157 distinct licence texts.
  Residual (Minor): 5 crates ship no licence file, so only their SPDX id is printed. MIT and BSD need the
  copyright line too. Add those texts by hand, or fall back to the standard text with the authors from metadata.

M7 stack strategy: FIXED (risk accepted, with evidence).
  The build scripts now explain why only Windows gets /STACK. mpp-render/tests/stack.rs lays out 250-deep fractions,
  250-deep powers and a 10 000-term sum on an 8 MB thread in debug.
  Residual: only page layout is covered. Library users on default 2 MB spawned threads, and the export
  writers (tex, pdf) walking deep trees on the main thread, are not tested. Low risk.

MINORS
------
m1  key echo: FIXED. rpassword at a terminal, read_line only when the key is piped in.
m2  keyring mock store: FIXED. apple-native on macOS and windows-native on Windows; elsewhere CredentialStore refuses
    (key.rs:107-123), so it never falls back to the in-memory mock.
m3  WiX XML escaping: FIXED. SecurityElement::Escape on Name and Source (files and folders).
m4  install-test.ps1: FIXED. ErrorActionPreference Stop, throws when no .msi, refuses to run when Math++ is installed, exit $fail.
m5  deny.toml: FIXED. [advisories] with both ignores and reasons (flagged NEEDS BEN). `cargo deny check` passes.
    Unicode-DFS-2016 dropped.
m6  mpp-cli links mpp-ide: NOT FIXED (Cargo.toml:23).
m7  forbid(unsafe_code): FIXED. All 9 library crates. There is no unsafe anywhere in crates/*/src or build.rs.
m8  build.rs duplication: NOT FIXED. The two files still differ only in 3 strings.
m9  empty std/: FIXED. mpp-eval/build.rs:27 emits cargo:warning.
m10 find_lake on every repaint: FIXED. find_lake_cached (10 s) at proof/ui.rs:639.
m11 TeX file commands: FIXED. neutralise_file_commands (tex.rs:271) covers \input, \openout, \write18,
    \immediate and others, with tests.
m12 god crate and holder order: NOT FIXED. mpp-core is now 59k lines. The holder order has two comments but no
    claimed-names mechanism. It was already marked "later".
m13 symlinks in agent file tools: FIXED. read_file canonicalizes the path and checks starts_with(folder)
    (tools.rs:273-278). A tiny check-then-read race is left, which doesn't matter here.

NEW FINDINGS IN FIX-ROUND CODE
------------------------------
N1 (MAJOR) The high-precision pi and ln 2 caches keep a cut-short value after a time-out or cancel. Later digits are then wrong, with no warning.
   crates/mpp-core/src/hp/float.rs:448-464 (cached) and 466-488 (atan_inv).
   atan_inv stops its series early when tick() fails ("the caller sees stopped() and discards the value").
   But cached() has already stored that cut-short sum in the thread-local PI / LN2 cell, under the full `want`
   precision. approx() throws away the one result that was computed while stopped. Any later pi(p) or ln2(p) on the
   same thread, with p up to `want`, gets the bad constant back. Nothing marks it as bad, so approx's
   two-precision agreement test "confirms" the wrong digits.
   Reproduced (scratch crate r3-recheck/pirepro, release): pi(400 000 bits) under a 1 ms budget, then the budget is
   dropped and pi(13 000 bits) is asked for on the same thread. It differs from a clean thread from decimal digit 2436 on.
   Where users can hit it: auto-eval pool workers are long-lived (auto.rs worker loop), so a job that times out
   or is cancelled while reducing a huge angle (sin of 10^30000, which needs pi to about 100k bits) poisons the next
   jobs on that worker. In `mpp run`, one document's queries share the eval thread, and budget::restart only
   resets the deadline. High-precision zero tests in checks (up to MAX_BITS = 16384) and approx up to 2000
   digits (about 27k bits) are inside the bad range.
   The trigger is narrow, but the result is a silently wrong answer.
   Fix (one line): in cached(), don't store the value when float::stopped() or budget::peek_tripped() is set,
   or make make_pi and make_ln2 return HpResult and pass Stopped up.
   Add a test: trip a budget during pi(big), then check pi(small) against a fresh thread.

N2 (Minor) improper.rs:312-336 tail() walks from c to 2·π·n0·2^8 in pieces of length π, so the cost grows with |c|.
   For |c| above about 1e16, x + π == x and the inner while loop never moves forward. Only budget::check stops it,
   and with no budget installed (library callers, and the IDE's explicit path has only cancel) it never ends.
   Not reached by \int_{10^{17}}^\infty \sin x/x^2 dx (another path answers it first), but nothing guards it.
   Fix: shift the start to the first whole period past c (the integral from c to there via adaptive), or cap the
   number of pieces and return None.
N3 (Minor) The IDE eval worker can lose a cancel. submit_file sets cancel and then sends the job. If the worker has
   already taken job N but not yet entered Session::update, update's `self.cancel.store(false)` (session.rs:390)
   wipes the cancel, and job N runs to the end before N+1 is seen. The IDE sets no time limit on explicit queries,
   so a slow N keeps every later result frozen (the M1 symptom, through a narrow window).
   Fix: use a generation counter instead of a bool, or clear the flag in the worker before it takes the job.
N4 (Minor) Deadlines overshoot inside hp. tick() runs every 512 series terms, and check() reads the clock every 64 calls,
   so about 32k terms pass between clock reads. pi(400 000 bits) under a 1 ms budget stopped after 2.2 s (release).
   It is bounded by REDUCE_BITS in practice, but the per-thread TICKS counter makes the gap hard to reason about.
   Read the clock on every hp tick (it is already rare).
N5 (Minor) hp/special.rs:24-52: the Bernoulli table is built up to index 800 with quadratic big-rational work and no tick().
   Related: approx(zeta(1/3), 2000 digits) took 9.4 s in release and returned None (no budget installed in the scratch run).
   Add tick() to the table loop.

Unwrap, expect and panic on user input: none new. With every #[cfg(test)] module, tests.rs, tests/ and testkit cut off,
the only hits are 3 unreachable!s. The two new ones in mpp-ide/src/editor/mod.rs:119 and 467 rest on an invariant
("the top undo or redo step keeps its whole text"), and every stack change goes through push_step and pop_step, so the invariant holds.
parser.rs:93 is the old one.
unsafe: none. Secrets: nothing new in the diff (no real keys). The key path is rpassword plus the refusing store.
Duplicated logic reintroduced: none beyond the rk4_system and plot-Newton leftovers noted under M4.

OUTSIDE MY SCOPE (pass to the maths reviewer)
- `$$ \int_{10^{17}}^{\infty} \frac{\sin x}{x^2} dx = ? $$` gives "≈ -6.599828355 × 10⁻³²". The true value is
  bounded by about 1/X² = 1e-34, so this is cancellation noise printed with 10 digits. 10^6 gives 9.37e-13, which is plausible.

TESTS
- Run 1: 1599 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored, 564 s wall including the build (was 1431 tests and 510 s at a6b9c78).
- Run 2 (no rebuild), checking for flaky tests: 1599 passed, 0 failed, 467 s wall. No flaky failures in two runs.
- Suites over 60 s (both runs): mpp-ide tests/m16.rs 112.6 s, mpp-export tests/golden.rs 81.9 s (was 72 s).
  Two tests each run over 60 s: every_example_exports_to_a_readable_pdf and
  every_example_has_a_screenshot_and_no_raw_source (same as before). Next slowest suite: 25.6 s.
- The timing tests (mpp-eval tests/limits.rs, 200-300 ms limits, and the IDE "live edit is fast" tests) passed in
  both runs while the machine was loaded.
- Suggest a test for N1 (pi or ln2 after a stopped computation) and one for N3 (cancel during job pick-up).
```

