  dark placeholder outline 3.25 (meets the 3:1 non-text rule), placeholder label 5.2.
- Grey auto-results; Ctrl+Enter writes the answer in; steps fold; right-click on a result → decimal / more / fewer digits;
  slider redraws; 3D orbit by drag; Prove… without a key explains and disables Start; export dialog writes a PDF.

==================================================================== TASTE (for Ben)
T1. Exact fractions for decimal input (9.81 + 1 = 1081/100). Most students want decimals back when they typed decimals.
T2. Explicit "= ?" answers are blue while auto-answers are grey. A printed paper would use black; consider grey for both, or black.
T3. Proof end mark is a filled ■ and "Proof." is bold. amsthm default is an open □ and italic "Proof.".
T4. Results use sin(x) / cos(x) with brackets while input reads sin x. Papers drop the brackets.
T5. Worked steps use text-size d/dx fractions. Display style would read like a textbook.
T6. Second view of a split is titled "waves (2)". VS Code repeats the plain name.
T7. Right-click on ordinary maths offers only "Insert…": no expand / factor / simplify reformat.
T8. Tutorial lesson 1 teaches typing ^ and / (keystrokes), while C9 says users never type ^. Fine as shortcuts; just make the wording consistent.
T9. "Unused" warnings on a worksheet's own definitions may be noise for students (maths files define things to read them).
T10. Explorer shows *.expected.txt files from examples. Consider hiding non-.mpp files, or keep them greyed as now.
