### Reviewer 5: performance and scale

#### Original review

```
M17 reviewer 5: performance and robustness
===========================================
Build: release, worktree review5 (detached), Windows 11. Every run was wrapped in a timeout.
Probes, generators and logs are in this folder (probes/, csv/, imp/, chain*/, dia*/, diac*/, out_*.txt, fuzz150k.log).
Temporary test files (not committed, delete before merging anything):
  crates/mpp-eval/tests/zz_r5_fuzz.rs, crates/mpp-eval/tests/zz_r5_probe.rs,
  crates/mpp-cli/tests/zz_r5_cli.rs, crates/mpp-ide/tests/zz_r5_ide.rs
Note: an early run reported false "hangs" on outputs over 4 KB. That was a pipe deadlock in my own runner,
since fixed. Every finding below comes from the fixed runner or from in-process timing.

BLOCKER (6)
-----------
B1. Nested definite integrals take exponential time: 10 levels hangs.
    Input:    $$ \int_0^1 \int_0^1 ... (n times) x \, dx ... \, dx = ? $$
    Observed: n=5 0.15 s, n=6 1.15 s (about 8x per level), n=10/50/200 still running at 30 s (in-process: HANG at 20 s).
              Memory stays flat (~12 MB): pure CPU spin with no budget check.
    Expected: about linear in n, or a clear "too large" error within seconds. The integrate engine has BUDGET/MAX_DEPTH,
              but the outer loop over nested integrals does not share it.
    Knock-on: in the IDE, a file like this in the same folder stalls every other file there (see M5).

B2. Math parser error recovery is quadratic on deep `^{`/`_{` nesting.
    Input:    $$ 2^{2^{2^{...1}}} = ? $$ with n levels (n ~ 1k–5k characters).
    Observed: parse_math alone takes n=1000 0.99 s, n=2000 3.8–4.2 s, n=5000 23.4 s; n=10000/100000 time out (> 30 s).
              parse_document is just as slow (it parses the maths). For comparison, plain "(" x 100000 parses in 8 ms.
    Expected: the "nested too deeply" error in milliseconds, the same as for parentheses.

B3. A 100k-row CSV takes about 12 s to load, and every live edit reloads it.
    Input:    csv/d100000.csv (100k rows, 2 columns, 4 MB) with "Data: $D$ from [[d100000.csv]]." and `x := 1`.
    Observed: load alone 12.3 s / 213 MB (10k rows 0.97 s, 30k 2.9 s: linear, about 0.12 ms per row).
              With the usual stats queries the full file takes 42.9 s / 547 MB.
              Session::update after a one-word prose edit takes 12.8–13.4 s every time. The CSV is not cached between updates.
    Expected: well under 1 s to load 100k numbers, and a cached import (keyed on mtime/len) for live edits.

B4. Exponential time and memory when an imported file with an error is reachable by more than one path.
    Input:    diac<N>/d0.mpp: N layers, each file imports the two files below it (d_{i+1} and e_{i+1});
              the bottom file has one syntax error (`z := 1 +`).
    Observed: N=16 0.28 s / 70 MB, N=18 0.85 s / 243 MB, N=21 14.8 s / 1.86 GB. Memory doubles per layer, so N=24
              would run out of memory. With no error the same graph runs in 0.17 s / 12 MB (so the evaluation itself is cached).
              The cost is in building the error summaries ("1 error in X: 1 error in Y: …"), which are built per path.
    Expected: each file's summary built once and shared. Also the count: "2 errors in d1.mpp" counts one error twice.

B5. The IDE evaluates every sibling .mpp file in the folder before the open file's results appear, so one slow sibling
    means no results for any file in that folder.
    Code:     diagnostics/background.rs FileCache::names_of / problems_of run Session::run_str on up to 64 sibling files
              (< 512 KB each) on the evaluation worker.
    Observed: confirmed in isolation: folder sib/ = bom.mpp (one line, 1+1) + intnest10.mpp. Opening bom.mpp gives no
              results: settle times out at 30 s, then a keystroke times out at another 30 s. bom.mpp alone settles in 0.8 ms.
    Also:     bom.mpp (one line) opened from probes/ (which also holds the B1/B2 files) did not settle in 30 s, and a keystroke
              then took another 30 s or more. The same file alone in its own folder settles in 0.8 ms.
    Expected: read names without evaluating (parse only), or index in the background with a budget, so a slow sibling
              never blocks the open file's results.

B6. A 10 MB single-line paragraph freezes the IDE.
    Input:    line10mb.mpp ("x" x 10,000,000, one line), alone in its own folder.
    Observed: open_file 14 ms, then the first settle and paint did not finish in 75 s (killed). `mpp run` handles the same file in 0.17 s.
    Expected: long lines laid out lazily or truncated, with the UI kept responsive.

MAJOR (9)
---------
M1. Typing in the IDE on a 3000-line document is above the 100 ms target.
    Input:    examples/long-worksheet.mpp body x10 (2984 lines, 65 KB), release build, egui_kittest, nothing else running.
    Observed: Session::update after a one-character edit: 31–47 ms (fine).
              IDE keystroke to evaluated and laid out: start 150 ms, middle 164 ms, end 243 ms, shared definition 235 ms.
              One frame on its own takes 43–86 ms (that frame is the keystroke, before the evaluation returns).
              Opening the document and letting it settle: 325 ms.
    Expected: under 100 ms (M16). The page relayout scales with document length; the evaluator does not.

M2. Mutual recursion between functions gives a wrong answer instead of an error.
    Input:    $$ f(x) := g(x) + 1 $$  $$ g(x) := f(x) + 1 $$  $$ f(1) = ? $$  $$ g(2) = ? $$
    Observed: "f(1) = g + 1" and "g(2) = 2g + 2" (g becomes a bare symbol). The plain variables a := b + 1, b := a + 1
              correctly report "circular definition: a → b → a".
    Expected: a circular-definition error, or "calls itself too deeply".

M3. Large exact results come back as "≈ ∞".
    Input / observed: 100000! = ?  → "100000! ≈ ∞";  2^{1000000} = ? → "≈ ∞";  ln(1000!) ≈ ? → "≈ ∞" (the true value is about 5912.13);
              2^{1000000} - 2^{1000000} = 0 is fine; mod(2^{1000000}, 7) → "2^1000000 is not an integer".
              2^{40000} = ? prints all 12k digits, while 2^{30000} ≈ ? gives "≈ ∞" (the float overflows above about 1.8e308).
    Expected: an exact query never answers with ∞. Show "≈ 2.824×10^456573" or "too large to show exactly", work in
              log space for ≈, and make mod work on big powers by modular exponentiation.

M4. Two IDE windows share one recovery folder, so a crash can lose recovery copies.
    Steps:    window A edits m.mpp and writes its recovery copy. Window B starts (its restore dialog offers A's live,
              uncrashed edits as "crash" copies: 1 file), then B closes cleanly → clear_recovery_on_exit() deletes the
              whole recovery folder. A's next write_recovery() skips the file because the revision has not changed, then A crashes.
    Observed: next start-up offers 0 files (expected 1). The unsaved edits are gone.
    Expected: a recovery folder per process (or a lock or PID in each copy), and clear only your own copies.
    (Test: zz_r5_ide.rs r5_two_windows_share_recovery.)

M5. One keystroke in a deeply nested (unclosed) expression costs 0.7 s and 1.5 GB in the IDE.
    Input:    paren100000.mpp (100k "(" pairs), alone in a folder. It opens and settles in 13 ms ("nested too deeply").
    Observed: one typed character takes 695 ms, with RSS 799 MB and a 1.48 GB peak. random_ascii.mpp (190 KB of junk maths):
              settle 1.15 s, one keystroke 1.6 s. pw2000.mpp (B2): settle 3.6 s.
    Expected: under 100 ms and bounded memory on input the parser has already rejected.

M6. A UTF-8 BOM directly before "$$" silently drops the first block.
    Input:    EF BB BF "$$ 1 + 1 = ? $$"
    Observed: `mpp run` prints nothing (exit 0) and `mpp check` says "ok". With a heading before it, the BOM is harmless.
    Expected: strip the BOM when reading (Notepad and other Windows tools write it).

M7. 100k-row CSV statistics: wrong or failing results, and exact rationals.
    Observed: corr(h, m) ≈ ? → "this needs too much work to evaluate";  ∑_i m_i = ? → "the term at i = 1 is undefined"
              (∑_i x_i works on a small in-document table);  var(m) = 37058421514852753/208331250000000 kg²;
              h̄ = 437529869/2500000 cm (data read from decimal CSV values shown as huge fractions);
              linreg works (p(170) ≈ 68.006).
    Expected: corr and ∑ work on measured data, and measured decimal data shows as decimals.

M8. One malformed 6 MB line uses 640 MB of RAM before it is rejected.
    Input:    $$ 1+1+…(3,000,000 terms) = ? $$
    Observed: 3.6 s and a 640 MB peak, then "this expression is too long; split it over several lines". 100k terms: 0.8 s / 32 MB.
    Expected: check the length before tokenising and building the tree.

M9. Recurrences stop at about 10k terms with a misleading message.
    Input:    a_0 := 1, a_{n+1} := a_n + 1, a_{50000} = ?  (and 99999, 10^6)
    Observed: a_{10000} = 10001 (0.09 s), but a_{50000} → "`a` calls itself too deeply". recurrence.rs allows
              MAX_TERMS = 100000 and computes terms iteratively, but the evaluator's recursive path cuts in first.
              Fibonacci a_{99999}: same error.
    Expected: terms up to MAX_TERMS computed iteratively (fast), and past that "term n is beyond the 100000-term limit".
              (User functions: f(1000) with depth 1001 → "calls itself too deeply" is a clear error, so Minor N1.)

MINOR (12)
----------
N1. MAX_CALL_DEPTH is 1000, so f(1000) for f(n) = f(n-1) + 1 fails (f(999) works). The error is clear but the limit is low for
    teaching recursion. Maybe memoise and recurse iteratively, or raise the limit (the evaluation already runs on a large stack).
N2. Error flood: one 260-deep unclosed bracket gives about 261 copies of "expected `)` to close the `(`" (8.8 KB of output).
    Report it once: "N unclosed brackets".
N3. The product error says "this sum is too large to compute" (∏_{k=1}^{10^9} (1 + 1/k²) ≈ ?).
N4. CR-only line endings (old Mac): the whole document is silently ignored (no output, "ok"). CRLF and mixed CRLF/LF work.
N5. Invalid UTF-8 .mpp or CSV: the whole file is refused ("stream did not contain valid UTF-8"). The message is clear, but one
    stray byte loses a whole data file. Consider reading lossily and flagging the bad line.
N6. An import chain deeper than 64 gives a 1.3 KB message "1 error in f1.mpp: 1 error in f2.mpp: … imports are nested more
    than 64 files deep", and a 100-file chain cannot be used at all. Shorten it to the root cause, and consider a higher limit.
N7. The recovery copy is written in place (std::fs::write, not write-to-temp then rename). A kill during the write leaves a
    truncated JSON, which load_all() silently skips, so that file's recovery is lost with no message. Tested: a half-written
    copy is not offered and start-up does not complain.
N8. Undo keeps up to 500 full-text snapshots per file (editor/mod.rs HISTORY_LIMIT). Memory over 600 keystrokes on the
    65 KB document grows 122 → 156 MB (about 57 KB per step, bounded at about 500 × document size). A 1 MB file could hold
    about 500 MB. Store diffs instead.
N9. When the Data file fails to load, later queries echo themselves ("h̄ = h̄", "median(m) = m") instead of
    "h is not defined (data file failed to load)".
N10. A non-identifier CSV header (c0 … c4999) rejects the whole file. The message is clear, but plain names like c0
     could be accepted.
N11. factor(2^{256}+1) takes 9.5 s before "no factor found in time". The answer is clear but close to the 10 s bound
     (the IDE worker is busy that long).
N12. 10 MB of "$" is echoed back in full as the "asked" text by `mpp run` (10 MB of stdout). Truncate long snippets.

PASSED / NO PROBLEM FOUND
-------------------------
- Fuzzing (proptest, zz_r5_fuzz.rs): 150,000 cases each on mpp_syntax::math::parse_math, document::parse_document and
  Session::run_str + update (token-based maths and document generators plus arbitrary Unicode; 10 s hang watchdog per case).
  0 panics, 0 hangs. Worst parse 35 ms; worst session 3.6 s on "Data: $D$ from [[m.csv]]." (once; CPU was contended,
  could not reproduce).
- Huge sums: ∑_{k=1}^{10^9} k exact in 0.09 s. Non-closed-form 10^9-term sums give a clear "too large" error.
  ∏_{k=1}^{100000} k = 100000! is symbolic. 3000! and 2^{40000} are exact and fast.
- Deep nesting of parentheses, braces, \frac and \sqrt to 100,000 levels: "nested too deeply" in ≤ 0.2 s, no stack overflow.
  Unary minus x 100k: fine. Lists, quotes and a 10 MB prose line: fine (≤ 0.5 s, ≤ 64 MB).
- CAS: expand((x+y+z)^200), (x+y+z+w)^50 and (x+1)^100000 give an instant "too large" error.
  factor of a degree-200 product works in 0.15 s. Nested-composition integrals give "no closed form" in ≤ 0.8 s
  (sin nested 200 deep: 5.3 s). 30th derivative of e^{sin x} tan x: 3 s (388 KB of output).
  lim (sin tan − tan sin)/x^7 = −1/30 is correct. 5x5 symbolic det: fine.
- Import loops (self, 2-cycle, 3-cycle): clear "import loop: a → b → a" errors. A name defined in two imports gives a
  clear message with a rename hint. Missing and binary imports give clear errors.
- Crash recovery with a real kill: a child process edits a file and keeps its recovery copy fresh; TerminateProcess
  mid-edit, then start-up offers 1 file and restores 25 of 25 edits (still marked unsaved). The file on disk is untouched
  with autosave off. Delay-mode autosave writes the file after 1 s idle. Corrupt recovery JSON does not crash start-up.
- Real binary (mathpp.exe, MPP_IDE_DATA redirected): it survives a hard kill and restart, garbage settings.json, corrupt and
  valid recovery copies, an invalid-UTF-8 file argument and a missing-file argument (alive after 8 s each, about 90–156 MB).
- Session memory is stable across repeated updates of the 3000-line document (25–30 MB).
