M17 reviewer 5 FINAL RE-CHECK: performance and robustness, final build
======================================================================
Build: fix/validation at 43b5960 (release). The worktree was not touched. Its only change, work/RUN-STATE.mpp,
was already there and is not mine. I copied the source to scratchpad\final\r5\src and added probe tests there:
crates/mpp-ide/tests/zz_r5_final.rs and crates/mpp-syntax/tests/zz_r5_memo.rs.
CARGO_TARGET_DIR is scratchpad\final\r5-target. CLI checks used scratchpad\final\mpp.exe. Every run had a timeout.
Logs in scratchpad\final\r5: lat3000.log, latfiles.log, stale.log, stale40.log, memo.log, memo_ide.log,
memo_ide_prose.log, undo.log, probe1-3.log, sweep_final.log (195 CLI probes; compare.py diffs it against the re-check logs).

NEW FINDINGS
------------
F1 MAJOR (regression from 169efaa, the parse memo): memory grows with every keystroke typed inside a long formula.
   parse_math keeps every formula text it has parsed, up to 20,000 entries per thread. The limit counts
   entries, not bytes, and each version of a formula is a new entry.
   In the IDE (one display formula of about 1.8 KB, typing inside it, settling every 10 keys), private memory was:
     1000 keys 120 MB, 2000 keys 220 MB, 3000 keys 327 MB, 4000 keys 432 MB, 5000 keys 538 MB.
     That is about 105 MB per 1000 keystrokes, and it keeps going.
   Control: the same document with the typing in prose stays flat (31 -> 51 MB after 3000 keys).
   On its own (mpp-syntax, one thread): 20,000 different 2 KB formulas take 2.1 GB private. That is the ceiling per
   thread, and the editor and evaluator threads each have their own memo. Formulas over 1500 tokens (about 6 KB
   here) are not kept, so a 6 KB or 12 KB formula stays flat at 6-7 MB. Formulas of about 0.5-3 KB are the worst case.
   Expected: limit the memo by bytes, not by entry count, or drop entries that are no longer in the current
   document after each parse.
F2 MINOR: the new "angle too large" error prints the whole angle when it is an exact integer.
   tan(10^{100000}) ≈ ? gives a 100,111-byte message ("the angle in tan(1000000…)"). cos(10^{50000}) gives 50 KB.
   10^{1000000} is shown in short form (sin(10^1000000)). Shorten the snippet, as was done for N12.
F3 MINOR: on a data column with a subscript name, \bar{c_1} = ? only echoes itself ("c₁̄ = c̄₁"). It should be the mean.
   mean(c_1) = 7/2 works, and \bar{h} works on plain names. (The c0… header itself is now accepted; see N10.)
F4 MINOR (not a regression: Doc::pump only re-evaluates when the text changes, and it did before too):
   when an imported file changes on disk, for example saved from another tab, the open file keeps its old answers
   until you type in it. Test: main imports lib.mpp ("a + 1 = ?" shows 11). Write a := 20 to lib.mpp and run frames
   for 2.5 s: it still shows 11. One keystroke in main gives 21. A rename through the app (move_item to
   lib2.mpp) rewrites the import and keeps 21, which is correct.

STALE-RESULT HUNT (new parse and evaluation caches): no stale result found
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Random edit fuzz (zz_r5_final.rs r5f_stale_fuzz). The document has an import, a function, an integral and
  inline maths. Edits were random inserts and deletes, undo (1-3 steps), redo, renaming a symbol everywhere
  (u_1 <-> w_2), changing the imported file on disk followed by an edit, and edits inside equations.
  Half the steps also laid out the keystroke frame with the older evaluation, which exercises the
  keep-the-page path. After settling, the live page was compared with a fresh Doc on a new thread
  (empty memo): maths text, character count, every problem's span and message, and every query answer.
  40 seeds x 80 steps = 3200 steps, 0 mismatches (another run: 480 steps, 0).
- Code read: memo.rs moves every Span the AST holds (nodes, units, labels, clauses, align points, diagnostics),
  and the parser has no global state. Editor revisions only go up, even on undo, so a cached (revision, parse)
  can never match different text. The page is reused only for the same revision and an equal fingerprint
  (eval_view). The link-existence cache is trusted for at most 1 s.
- Shipped tests pass in release: robustness_ui 9/9 (including the junk test with its 100 ms limit, layout and
  problem reuse matching a fresh layout, and keep-page or re-layout). mpp-syntax's document_fuzz and the memo
  unit test pass. (repository_files_parse_without_panics failed only because my copy has no work/ folder.)

